+### DeepFloyd IF 2D Guidance
+A text-to-image guidance approach using DeepFloyd IF, which features an Imagen-like architecture and a T-5 text encoder for stronger text understanding [S8]. It downsamples the input image to 64×64 pixels before passing it to DeepFloyd IF due to its modular cascaded design [S8]. It produces better text-3D alignment than StableDiffusion due to the T-5 encoder, but yields blurrier assets because of the mandatory 64×64 downsampling [S8].
+
+### Coherent Score Distillation Sampling
+A method incorporating fine-grained language features through MaskCLIP and FeatUp to enable open-vocabulary querying [S9]. It introduces Coherent Score Distillation Sampling (CSD) to ensure edits remain consistent across multiple views [S9].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** The method requires multiple forward passes through the diffusion UNet per optimization step (one for conditional, one for unconditional, or batched). The complexity is dominated by the UNet inference, which is $O(N_{params} \cdot H \cdot W)$ where $N_{params}$ is the number of parameters in the UNet, and $H, W$ are the latent dimensions [S1, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the UNet weights, the VAE encoder/decoder weights, and the intermediate activations for the backward pass. The latent space reduces memory usage compared to pixel-space diffusion [S1, unofficial].
-- **Measured Performance:** Performance depends heavily on the `guidance_scale` and timestep selection. High guidance scales (e.g., 100) can lead to over-saturation or artifacts if not balanced with proper timestep scheduling [S1, unofficial].
-- **Scaling:** Scales with the resolution of the rendered image (or latent size). Latent-space diffusion allows for efficient optimization at high effective resolutions (e.g., 512x512) without the memory cost of pixel-space diffusion [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Leverages powerful 2D diffusion priors for high-quality texture and geometry generation. Flexible with various conditioning inputs (text, masks, sketches) [S1, unofficial][S2, S3, S4].
-- **Limitations:** Can suffer from "Janus" problems (multi-headed objects) or floaters if not properly regularized. Computationally expensive due to the need for multiple UNet passes and gradient computation through the diffusion process [S2, S6].
-- **When to use:** When generating 3D content from 2D text or image prompts is required, and high-fidelity texture is a priority.
-- **When not to use:** When real-time performance is critical, or when explicit 3D supervision is available and sufficient.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Each training step requires a forward pass through the radiance field, a VAE encode, a UNet forward pass (twice for CFG), and a backward pass through the UNet and VAE to compute gradients for $\theta$. This is computationally expensive, typically requiring many steps (e.g., 1000-2000) for convergence [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the radiance field parameters, the frozen diffusion model weights (UNet, VAE, Text Encoder), and intermediate latents. The UNet forward pass dominates memory usage [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** SDS has demonstrated remarkable results in Text-to-3D generation [S1]. However, it is known to encounter issues such as over-saturation and over-smoothing [S1]. Direct application on NeRF renders may produce blurred details and fail to converge on detailed reconstructions due to local patch optimization [S1].
+*   **Scaling:** Performance scales with the capacity of the diffusion model and the number of training steps. Variants like RSD aim to improve detail generation [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Enables text-to-3D generation without 3D training data; leverages powerful 2D priors.
+*   **Limitations:** Prone to over-saturation, over-smoothing, and Janus problems (inconsistent geometry across views); computationally intensive; sensitive to hyperparameters like guidance scale and timestep range [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** When generating 3D assets from text prompts is required and multi-view data is unavailable.
+*   **When not to use:** When high geometric consistency across views is critical without additional multi-view guidance (e.g., [MVDream](6345-mvdream-multi-view-diffusion-guidance.md)); when computational resources are limited.
 
 ## Background
-Originated from the DreamFusion paper by Poole et al. (2022), which introduced Score Distillation Sampling (SDS) as a method to optimize 3D representations using pre-trained 2D diffusion models [S1, unofficial][S6]. The method was developed to address the lack of 3D data for training generative models by leveraging the vast amount of 2D data used to train models like Stable Diffusion. It has since become a foundational technique in text-to-3D generation and has been extended in numerous ways, including multi-view guidance, inpainting, and style transfer [S1, unofficial][S2, S3, S4, S5, S6].
+Score Distillation Sampling (SDS) was introduced by [Poole et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.14742) in 2022 as an optimization objective that uses the score function of pretrained diffusion models to guide the optimization of a differentiable image parametrization [S1]. It has since become a foundational technique for Text-to-3D generation, enabling methods like DreamFusion [S1]. The method relies on the equivalence between the noise prediction in diffusion models and the score function $\nabla_{z_t} \log p(z_t | y)$ [S1]. Subsequent work has addressed its limitations, such as over-smoothing and lack of multi-view consistency, leading to variants like Renoised SDS, Interval Score Matching, and Coherent SDS [S1, S3, S9].
 
